01:54:00.350 --> 01:54:06.280
Claudette Sims. Welcome to our program. You know, one doesn't have to be a genius to figure out that

01:54:06.280 --> 01:54:11.500
the African-American community is in a state of crisis. Why? Because the black family is in a state

01:54:11.500 --> 01:54:17.120
of crisis. Why? Because the African-American male is in the state of crisis. True, there are some

01:54:17.120 --> 01:54:22.020
success stories. Black families, thousands of husbands and wives and children were succeeding

01:54:22.020 --> 01:54:27.000
against the odds. But an overwhelming number of black families still live in poverty in single

01:54:27.000 --> 01:54:29.980
parent homes headed by black women without the moral

01:54:30.000 --> 01:54:35.310
emotional, or financial support of black men. And where are they? Many of them are unemployed,

01:54:35.830 --> 01:54:41.000
underemployed, in drugs, or in jail. We know the stats. The more important question is what can we

01:54:41.000 --> 01:54:45.220
do about it? And can we do it before we lose another generation of black men?

01:54:45.640 --> 01:54:52.550
Eyewitness News reporter Don Cobos follows this report. They're being called the endangered

01:54:52.550 --> 01:54:57.210
African-American male. We're talking about black youth in elementary and high schools

01:54:57.210 --> 01:54:59.970
who end up in non-traditional and unproductive.

01:55:00.190 --> 01:55:06.370
areas of crime and drugs. Young black males, we think that every aspect of society has problems

01:55:06.370 --> 01:55:12.230
for this particular group. Dr. Wadpage is worried about the 38,000 black youth in the HISD schools.

01:55:12.660 --> 01:55:17.880
He cites recent nationwide studies that indicate that 23 percent of black males are involved in our

01:55:17.880 --> 01:55:22.960
criminal justice system in this country, that black males suffer more health problems, and that black

01:55:22.960 --> 01:55:27.940
males are suspended from school three times more than white males, and the list goes on. Another

01:55:27.940 --> 01:55:29.900
concern centers around the small number

01:55:30.000 --> 01:55:34.620
of black male role models that young blacks find in our public school systems.

01:55:35.180 --> 01:55:39.160
One nationwide survey recently indicated that only 1.2% of the

01:55:39.160 --> 01:55:44.390
elementary school teachers and 3.2% of the high school teachers are black males.

01:55:44.980 --> 01:55:48.480
One program that's attacking this problem has roots in African history and

01:55:48.480 --> 01:55:52.600
culture. Reverend Bill Lawson of Wheeler Avenue Baptist Church is turning to

01:55:52.600 --> 01:55:57.100
what he's called the rites of passage. Where the men of an African village would

01:55:57.100 --> 01:55:59.880
take the boys of that African village would spend

01:56:00.000 --> 01:56:03.760
some time teaching them the skills of adulthood.

01:56:04.260 --> 01:56:08.440
Lawson has started a related program with the young boys who are members of his church.

01:56:09.080 --> 01:56:11.860
School board member Melody Ellis agrees with Lawson's idea.

01:56:12.280 --> 01:56:14.760
There's nowhere in the curriculum that tells you about

01:56:14.760 --> 01:56:19.900
African-American males who have done wonderful things in our communities,

01:56:19.900 --> 01:56:22.020
and our black youth need to know that.

01:56:22.240 --> 01:56:24.180
Meanwhile, at Thursday's school board meeting,

01:56:24.380 --> 01:56:29.460
Dr. Page asked Superintendent Joan Raymond to explore the idea of putting together a related program.

01:56:30.000 --> 01:56:33.240
To save black youth, this growing, endangered minority.

